            <ArticleTitle>Morphine blocks descending pain inhibitory controls in humans.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In man, heterotopic painful thermal conditioning stimuli induce parallel decreases in the spinal nociceptive flexion (RIII) reflex and the concurrent sensation of pain elicited by electrical stimulation of the sural nerve at the ankle. Such phenomena may be related to the diffuse noxious inhibitory controls (DNIC) which were initially described in the rat and subsequently documented in humans. In 9 subjects in the present study, a 2 min application of a moderately noxious temperature (46 degrees C) to the contralateral hand strongly depressed the RIII reflex elicited in the biceps femoris muscle by electrical stimulation of the sural nerve at 1.2 times the reflex threshold. These depressive effects were maximal during the second min of the conditioning period, showing a 80% inhibition of the RIII reflex which gradually recovered to its baseline value 7 min after the end of the conditioning period. Such inhibitory effects were completely blocked 15-26 min after administration of a low dose of morphine hydrochloride (0.05 mg/kg, i.v.). The lifting of the inhibitions was compatible with an action at the opioid receptors since the inhibitions were re-observed 5-16 min after naloxone injection (0.006 mg/kg, i.v.). During all the experimental sessions, heart and respiratory rates remained stable at their control levels. Since it has been shown previously that such a dose of morphine could not have a direct effect within the spinal cord (Willer 1985), it is concluded that this opiate blocks, in a naloxone-reversible fashion, those bulbo-spinal controls which are triggered by heterotopic nociceptive events. Possible implications for hypoalgesia based on the principles of counter-irritation are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
